<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>The short version</h2>
    <p>
        EggLedger reads your mission history from the Egg, Inc. API. There is no advertising, no
        third-party tracking, and no analytics. What is stored, and where, depends on which build
        you use. The desktop app keeps your mission data on your own machine. The hosted web app
        decodes it on the server and keeps it under your account until it is deleted. Payment data
        is never handled here.
    </p>
    <DesktopOnly>
        <p>You are running the desktop app, so the Desktop app section below applies to you.</p>
    </DesktopOnly>
    <WebOnly>
        <p>You are using the hosted web app, so the Web app section below applies to you.</p>
    </WebOnly>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Signing in</h2>
    <p>
        Sign-in is handled by EggIdentity, a separate federated authentication service run by the
        same operator. You pick a sign-in source: Discord, Google, Microsoft, or GitHub. Your
        password is never seen or stored by EggLedger.
    </p>
    <p>
        EggLedger receives only the basic identity needed to recognise you again, such as an
        account identifier, a display name, and an avatar. It does not read your email, messages,
        or server list. EggIdentity has its own
        <a href="https://eggidentity.davidarthurcole.me/terms" target="_blank" rel="noopener">terms</a>
        and
        <a href="https://eggidentity.davidarthurcole.me/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">privacy policy</a>
        covering the login itself.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Desktop app</h2>
    <p>
        The desktop build stores all config, mission data, and exports in the same directory as
        the EggLedger binary, in a local SQLite database and on-disk files. Nothing is uploaded
        anywhere. The only outbound network calls are to the Egg, Inc. API itself and an
        occasional update check against github.com, which carries no personal data. Unless you
        contact the operator directly or turn on Settings Sync, there is no way to know you are
        even using this tool.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Web app</h2>
    <p>
        The hosted instance runs as a Blazor Server app. Your browser holds a live connection to
        the server while you use it, and your Egg, Inc. backup is decoded server-side. The result
        is stored in a Postgres database, tied to your account. What is stored is:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Mission history.</strong> The spaceship missions your fetches return.</li>
        <li><strong>Drops.</strong> The loot recorded against each mission.</li>
        <li><strong>Backups.</strong> The decoded account snapshot each fetch is built from.</li>
        <li><strong>Settings.</strong> The interface and export preferences you have chosen.</li>
        <li><strong>Reports.</strong> The report definitions and groups you have built.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        That data is yours. It is not sold, shared, or used for advertising, and it is not
        profiled. It is kept until it is deleted, as described below. If you would rather nothing
        was stored server-side, use the desktop app instead.
    </p>
    <p>
        Separately from your account, the server logs API requests that it rejects, recording the
        IP address, path, and user agent, so that abuse can be blocked. Those rows carry no account
        identifier and are not joined to your data.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Settings Sync (optional)</h2>
    <p>
        Settings Sync lets you carry your settings between devices. On desktop it is off unless
        you turn it on, and turning it on requires signing in. If you never use it, nothing about
        you leaves your machine beyond the Egg, Inc. API calls and the update check described
        above.
    </p>
    <p>
        Synced settings are encrypted before upload and stored encrypted at rest. The key is
        generated per account by the sync service and handed to your devices when you sign in, so
        this is not a zero-knowledge design: the operator's infrastructure holds the key and can
        technically read what you sync. The web app uses that same key to restore your settings on
        your first sign-in. No settings data passes through the sign-in source you picked.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Cookies</h2>
    <p>
        The web app uses only strictly-necessary cookies. There are no advertising or tracking
        cookies, so there is no consent banner to click through. The cookies used are:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Session cookie.</strong> Keeps your Blazor Server circuit and your sign-in
            connected across page loads. Removed when you log out or close the session.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Antiforgery token cookie.</strong> A security cookie that protects form
            submissions against cross-site request forgery. It does not identify you.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        The web app does not keep your preferences in the browser. They are saved server-side in
        your account's settings, along with the rest of your stored data. The desktop app uses no
        cookies; its settings live in the on-disk config described above.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Payments</h2>
    <p>
        Donations are processed entirely by third-party platforms (GitHub Sponsors, Patreon, and
        Buy Me a Coffee). This application never sees, processes, or stores your payment card
        data. Anything to do with billing is held by those platforms under their own privacy
        policies.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Data retention</h2>
    <p>
        Desktop data is retained for as long as you keep it on disk. Deleting the app's directory
        removes it completely, with no system-level traces left behind. Web app data is retained
        until it is deleted, either by you through the options below or by the operator on request;
        there is no scheduled purge, and no history of your activity is built for profiling
        purposes. Synced settings are kept only while your account exists. The rejected-request log
        described above is kept for abuse protection and is not tied to any account.
    </p>
</section>

<section class="legal-section">
    <h2>Removing your data</h2>
    <p>To remove the data stored about you:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Desktop.</strong> Delete the EggLedger install directory to remove all local
            data. Use <strong>Delete Remote Data</strong> in the cloud sync settings to remove the
            synced settings held for you on the server.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Web.</strong> Use <strong>Disconnect</strong> in the cloud sync settings to
            delete your synced settings and sign out. This does not remove the mission data stored
            under your account.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Your whole account.</strong> There is no self-service button for this yet. Ask
            the operator and the account is deleted along with everything stored under it: mission
            history, drops, backups, settings, and reports.
        </li>
        <li>
            Revoke EggLedger's access from your sign-in source, such as your Discord, Google,
            Microsoft, or GitHub account settings.
        </li>
        <li>
            Clear your browser's cookies for the web app to remove the session and antiforgery
            items.
        </li>
        <li>
            Reach the operator through the
            <a href="https://github.com/EggIncTools/EggLedger" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub repository</a>
            or the project
            <a href="https://discord.davidarthurcole.me" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Discord server</a>.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>

